47
00:02:33,997 --> 00:02:37,602
And Abimelech, a stranger in
his own city, blesses God.

48
00:02:38,052 --> 00:02:39,695
Could you? Tell us below.

49
00:02:41,645 --> 00:02:44,649
Did you know? The exile
is usually counted as

50
00:02:44,649 --> 00:02:46,786
seventy years. So why sixty‑six?

51
00:02:47,236 --> 00:02:52,005
In 1889 the scholar Rendel
Harris proposed a hidden date.

52
00:02:52,005 --> 00:02:55,769
The Temple fell a second
time in the year 70.

53
00:02:56,219 --> 00:03:00,076
Seventy plus sixty‑six
is 136: just after Rome

54
00:03:00,076 --> 00:03:03,430
barred the Jewish people from Jerusalem.

55
00:03:03,880 --> 00:03:07,513
An old exile, retold for a
new one. Other scholars call

56
00:03:07,513 --> 00:03:10,749
that date too neat. Send
this to a history lover.

57
00:03:12,699 --> 00:03:15,768
An angel leads Abimelech
to Baruch, Jeremiah's

58
00:03:15,768 --> 00:03:17,969
scribe, who is sitting in a tomb.

59
00:03:18,419 --> 00:03:21,329
Baruch sees the basket
and weeps. Sixty‑six

60
00:03:21,329 --> 00:03:23,901
years old, and not one fig shrivelled.

61
00:03:24,351 --> 00:03:27,871
He speaks to his own body: "So
it will be with you, my flesh."

62
00:03:28,321 --> 00:03:31,281
For Baruch the figs are
proof. What God keeps

63
00:03:31,281 --> 00:03:33,847
does not decay, and the dead will rise.

64
00:03:34,297 --> 00:03:37,432
Then he ties a letter and
fifteen figs to an eagle's

65
00:03:37,432 --> 00:03:40,206
neck. Don't be like Noah's
raven, he tells it.

66
00:03:40,656 --> 00:03:43,484
In Babylon, the eagle
lands on a dead man at

67
00:03:43,484 --> 00:03:45,733
